**Date:** 28 October 2026 · **Decider:** Korred · **Scope:** sched-prod reports in the 4 October export.

**Supersedes [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject).** The old record stays intact as history.
The [incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says “one issue, one fix queued”; its computed views show that this conclusion is incomplete.

## What the desk can establish

The shared parser reads month first even when the tenant writes day first. Separately, booking-v3
prints a month-first hint while its form reads the tenant's day-first order. Those are different
paths and need separate fixes. The form-hint problem affects Alder Physio, Copper Studio and Pale Harbour.

<ReviewedReports />

Count distinct report IDs, not export rows: overlapping export pages repeat reports.
Use the tenant directory's locale and form together with the channel, as `causeOf` does;
browser locale and `meta.intakeTag` are intake guesses. The table exposes disagreements between
those guesses and the tenant-based classification below. The issues in this repository record each
group's count and evidence; these counts describe this export, not a live queue.

- [Parser issue #1](https://github.com/Korred/mega-dev-tasks/issues/1).
- [Form-hint issue #2](https://github.com/Korred/mega-dev-tasks/issues/2).
- Separate investigations: [R-41202 · #3](https://github.com/Korred/mega-dev-tasks/issues/3),
  [R-41209 · #4](https://github.com/Korred/mega-dev-tasks/issues/4),
  [R-41236 · #5](https://github.com/Korred/mega-dev-tasks/issues/5),
  [R-41344 · #6](https://github.com/Korred/mega-dev-tasks/issues/6),
  [R-41620 · #7](https://github.com/Korred/mega-dev-tasks/issues/7).

## What the original classifier shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

This original component assigns by tenant and channel without reading the body. Its parser
total includes the unresolved reports. The reviewed table above holds them apart; both tables
cover the same distinct reports. A computed label is a hypothesis when the words contradict it.

## Evidence that still needs investigation

The original table's parser group includes the desk's one-day notes. Each has its own
investigation issue and is excluded from the reviewed parser count. Their words do not prove
a month/day swap or establish that all five share a cause:

- **R-41202:** 12 May appears as 11 May, with a client arriving on the earlier day.
- **R-41209:** every June booking is said to be one day off.
- **R-41236:** the invite is said to arrive before the booking was made; no date pair is given.
- **R-41344:** the same meeting shows Tuesday in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto.
- **R-41620:** Tuesday 6 October appears as Monday, and the reporter says they have stopped reporting.

Check original booking input, stored date/time, timezone, invite payload and recipient display
before calling these a timezone bug or another confirmed cause. Do not promise that either known
fix resolves them. Keep these reports in separate investigation issues now. “Unknown” is not
a shared cause. Merge investigations later only when evidence establishes the same problem,
and recompute the affected counts without double counting. Waiting for a root cause before
opening an issue would leave these reports hidden behind a queued parser fix.

## Received is different from merged

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports arrived from April onward. The desk did not merge any into this ticket until July;
the large July merge bar includes earlier reports. That bar measures desk activity, not a sudden
July onset. New reports continue into October. “Known” and “queued” do not establish that a fix
shipped or that people stopped being affected. The final report also warns that silence can be
under-reporting, rather than recovery.

## Decision: merge only with evidence of the same cause

Read the body, look up the tenant, and inspect the channel and booking form before merging.
Matching subjects can describe different problems; different subjects can describe the same
problem. Intake labels are supporting clues, never the deciding key.

<DedupeExplorer />

The explorer applies the supplied tenant-based model. Its cases show why a subject-only rule
fails: the same subject can separate by channel or tenant, and different subjects can share a cause.
A tenant missing from the directory has no established cause: keep the report separate and
investigate. Two missing causes are not proof of a duplicate. Also keep reports separate when
their bodies contradict the model or do not contain enough evidence, as the notes above illustrate.

## What changes for the desk

Keep separate parser and form-hint tickets plus one investigation ticket for each unresolved
report, retaining report IDs, channel, tenant and
observed input/output. Ask for reproduction details where missing. Verify each fix on the affected
path before closing its ticket; closing one must not close the other.

This replaces the instruction to merge when unsure. It costs more reading up front, but prevents
one queued fix from hiding a different problem. Recheck this export against fresh reports before
using its counts for current staffing or incident status. The shared page frame displays age and
warns when the review date becomes stale; that warning is a prompt to check, not evidence that
the underlying claims are current.

